A||||34||grnmonkey6||Are Fiero's Safe?||||1||
Z||000000||grnmonkey6||12-13-2001||11:22 PM||||I've posted this before, but am still looking for more info:<P>I have been interested in buying a fiero for a long time now, but have not because i have always assumed they are quite unsafe, (i hope this is untrue, for i would really like to get one).  This may be untrue, but i cannot find anything about Fiero safety on the web, or anywhere else. <P>Does anyone know where i can find something on fiero crash tests, or any other websites or anything on fiero safety?||||reg||1
Z||000001||CountFiero||12-13-2001||11:46 PM||||Fieros are just a "safe" as ANY other car.  It all depends on how SANE the driver is.  I've put over 100k on mine and never had any safety concerns.  But then, I don't drive it like a "nutcase".  From what I've read over the years the frame on Fieros is one of the STRONGEST ever produced.  I know one driver that managed to roll his over on a turn--as impossible as that sounds, with the Fieros LOW center of gravity, and literally he walked away without a scratch.  I have read all of the bulletins about fires in some of the early models but  that was all addressed with recalls.  Obviously there are exceptions to every rule but there are No fundamental design flaws to be overly concerned with.  Drive it like you would any other car, safe and sane, and it will be a "pleasure" to own.  Just be aware that they are ALL "older" cars NOW and they need alot of TLC.  But for most of us that is part of the joy of owning what is a "classic" in the making.  Mine STILL turns ALOT of heads when I drive it.  Part of the fun of driving it is watching the expressions on other drivers faces.  I think that ALOT of people wanted one when they first came out in '84 but just couldn't afford it or put it off because of it was "impractical"  but you can see it in their faces they still regret not having bought one.  My advice...get one while you STILL can.  The popularity is growing and so are the clubs.  When that happens, prices and availibility reflect the trend.  They are<BR>still affordable but they are becoming increasingly popular with kids who love to "modify" the basic design.  Just read the posts.  Fieros are STILL....COOOOOOL  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000002||VeeSixGeeTee||12-14-2001||12:01 AM||||Id say yes...I feel very safe in mine..im sure the space frame helps a lot..its kinda like a roll cage  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> anyway..If ya don't drive crazy..and look out for other drivers..you should be fine. Have Fun!<P>------------------<BR>Mike<BR>85 GT 4sp(Sleeping)<BR>If everything seems to be going well, you have obviously overlooked something.||||reg||1
Z||000003||recluse||12-14-2001||12:01 AM||||I was doing 65 mph 4 seconds before my car ended up here.  Walked away with only a sore neck... im convinced they are safe.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/newspaper2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Adrift||12-14-2001||12:03 AM||||Just don't get rear-ended and you'll be fine - as many people on this board will tell you in crash tests the only car that out performed the fiero was a volvo - but then again, these crash tests are over 10 years old, and crash tests are head-on...<P>I remember being told the original beetle had a big problem with "blowing up" if they were rear ended because the engine was in the back - does anyone know if this applies to fieros? I mean logically if your engine is in the back, and someone smashes into you, hoses on the engine with oil and what not could be cut thereby setting the rear of your car on fire (this is all guessing on my part - for my own saftey, please correct me!) ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Mach10||12-14-2001||12:22 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Adrift:<BR><B>Just don't get rear-ended and you'll be fine - as many people on this board will tell you in crash tests the only car that out performed the fiero was a volvo - but then again, these crash tests are over 10 years old, and crash tests are head-on...<P>I remember being told the original beetle had a big problem with "blowing up" if they were rear ended because the engine was in the back - does anyone know if this applies to fieros? I mean logically if your engine is in the back, and someone smashes into you, hoses on the engine with oil and what not could be cut thereby setting the rear of your car on fire (this is all guessing on my part - for my own saftey, please correct me!) </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>What?!??!?? Oh, COME ON. Don't front-end anyone in a FWD car, or it'll explode.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif"> There isn't anything to EXPLODE in the back, except 600lbs of cast-iron.<P>the problem is related to placement of fuel tank... In the fiero, if you get hit hard enough to rupture it, you're DEAD. The other car's bumper is sitting on the center-console. Don't worry about it.<P>The fiero is PERFECTLY safe. Only thing you have to worry about is getting hit by something BIG like a semi... But then, even an SUV wouldn't save you...<P>As was mentioned, Only volvo tested higher, by *ONE POINT!* If I remember correctly, in an offset-crash, there was 10-odd extra pounds of pressure detected on one of the legs of the driver dummies... Take that as you will.||||reg||12
Z||000006||88-DOHC||12-14-2001||12:57 AM||||Exactly!  Some people get concerned when they find out the fuel tank is right beside where they sit. Little do they realize that is the safest place for the fuel tank, in the middle of the car.  Old pintos had problems in this regard because the fuel tank was in the rear and kind of exposed.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Oreif||12-14-2001||01:12 AM||||There are many stories of Fiero's involved in accidents and the people walking away.<BR>A member of our local club a few years back was T-boned by a full size chevy pick-up. Some bruises and a fractured rib was all he got. (The pick-up driver spent 2 weeks in a hospital because he wasn't wearing his seat belt) Last year some kid drove his dad's suburban thru traffic in Denver. The Fiero was "run over" by a car pushed into it by the truck (which was doing 75mph). The owner of the car was hurt very bad but still alive. Do a search for "anthony barber" in the general archives and you'll see the pics. The Fiero was only 1 point behind the Volvo in side impact tests. The 2 cars scored the same in front impacts.<BR>The problem is people who do not understand the space frame design think because the body panels are plastic that it's a deathtrap. Most people don't realize the plastic panels are only "covers" and have no effect on the structural integrity.<P>------------------<BR>             <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">           <BR><FONT SIZE="1"><B><I>Happiness isn't around the corner...<BR>Happiness IS the corner.</I></B></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000008||JetroGT||12-14-2001||03:48 AM||||I know I have more crash test pics, I just have to find them. This is a 1984 NHTSA test.<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/NHTSA1984.jpg">  <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/View[3].jpg"> <P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/NewSigt.jpg">    <BR>I work where all the missing paperclips go.<p>[This message has been edited by JetroGT (edited 12-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||GTDude||12-14-2001||07:55 AM||||My family owned a Pontiac dealership back in the 80's and early 90's......actually since 1939, but the point is...Many magazines of the time reported the Fiero as the SAFEST small car of the time due to the complete steel body "draped" with plastic.<P>Personally I've been in a nasty wreck in a Fiero back in 1984...it was a new demo and I was much younger....LOL.  It mashed the front end all the way up to the windshield, but no further.  Since then, I've been a big believer in Fiero safety.  I'm a much better driver now and more cautious.  For the size car, I'm not sure that there is a new car out there of the same size that is safer.  <P>------------------<BR>GTDude's 86GT||||reg||3
Z||000010||fieroman_5000||12-14-2001||08:07 AM||||My dad has totaled 3 Fieros in their era('84SE, 85GT, 88GT) The worst thing that happened to him was that he got a "golf ball" sized bruise on his left arm . In all those accidents that has all that happened<P>Fieroman <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||12
Z||000011||84Bill||12-14-2001||08:34 AM||||Nope they are not safe!<BR>Just deliver it to me and I'll dispose of it properly  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000012||Raydar||12-14-2001||09:45 AM||||This was a parts car I bought from some people last year.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Wreck1.jpg"> <BR>Was driven into a ditch when the driver blew a turn in the rain.<BR>She was uninjured. The passenger side of the car was over a foot shorter than the driver's side. The right front wheelwell and floor were only pushed back about 2-4 inches. Although it was totaled, I was still able to drive it into my garage under its own power. <BR>These things are built like anvils. Very solid.   <P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>From the Department of Redundancy Department.</FONT><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 12-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||Fie Ro||12-14-2001||09:47 AM|||| <IMG SRC="http://homepage.mac.com/rewq/images/crash/inspect.jpg"> <P>If this wasn't a Fiero I would be dead!      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P><p>[This message has been edited by Fie Ro (edited 12-14-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000014||Patrick's Dad||12-14-2001||10:01 AM||||&lt;quote&gt;Only volvo tested higher, by *ONE POINT!* If I remember correctly, in an offset-crash, there was 10-odd extra pounds of pressure detected on one of the legs of the driver dummies... Take that as you will.&lt;/quote&gt;<P>I'd heard that only a M/B had beaten the Fiero in these tests.... Oh, well.<P>A friend of mine related a story of how her brother in law's Fiero had been stolen from him, and, months later was involved in a high speed chase. The driver hit a sidewalk at nearly 100MPH and flipped the car. It rolled three times before stopping. The driver then opened the door, got out, and ran away from the police.<P>I also recall a Pontiac commercial in which a TranSport's bare frame (A spaceframe design based on the Fiero) was shown supporting two more TransSports on top of it.<P>RE: the fuel tank placement: This is also not safe if being filmed by an NBC news crew....||||reg||1
Z||000015||Raydar||12-14-2001||10:06 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Fie Ro:<BR><B> If this wasn't a Fiero I would be dead!       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That was you?!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><BR>I've seen those pics. Glad you're still with us! <P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>From the Department of Redundancy Department.</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000016||Indiana_resto_guy||12-14-2001||10:09 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Fie Ro:<BR><B>[IMG]<A HREF="http://homepage.mac.com/rewq/images/crash/inspect.jpg" TARGET=_blank>http://homepage.mac.com/rewq/images/crash/inspect.jpg</A>[/IMG]<P>If this wasn't a Fiero I would be dead!      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P><BR>[This message has been edited by Fie Ro (edited 12-14-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Ouch!<BR>Looks like a tree or pole @ 50 MPH (70-85 KPH?)<BR>Glad you made it! I have one almost identical to it in the barn, The front bumper on it moved 10 inches less @ 40 MPH.<BR>No injuries.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000017||Fie Ro||12-14-2001||10:12 AM||||Yeah that was me. No permanent damage to MY body....but the Fiero....aaah<P>I still have the Fierovirus though   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>...and another Fiero   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Yes Indiana, that was a tree at 50MPH<p>[This message has been edited by Fie Ro (edited 12-14-2001).]||||reg||12
Z||000018||Turbo88||12-14-2001||11:27 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by recluse:<BR><B>I was doing 65 mph 4 seconds before my car ended up here.  Walked away with only a sore neck... im convinced they are safe.<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/newspaper2.jpg">  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>HOLY SHYTE! Thats rather impressive!||||reg||1
Z||000019||StuGood||12-14-2001||12:36 PM||||Fieros seem to be very tough, and fare very well in collisions.  The fuel tank is extremely well-protected.  They are pretty nimble, so collisions can be avoided.  <P>On the other hand, they are hard for other drivers to see.  You have to be on your guard all the time (of course, the same is true for any car).<P>They are very repairable, based on my experiences.<P>But-!  Fieros can be a real hazard to bystanders, because when you are working on them, you tend to throw tools  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  Just kidding... ||||reg||12
Z||000020||Pheagey||12-14-2001||12:59 PM||||[QUOTE]Originally posted by Fie Ro:<BR>[B]  <IMG SRC="http://homepage.mac.com/rewq/images/crash/inspect.jpg">  <BR>Dddd*******mmmmmmmmmmnnnnnnnnnn boy! {8-O !!!How fast where going? Poor fiero... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>------------------<BR>'88 coupe, 5 speed, custome paint job<BR>"I! have a job!!!"<BR>Have you seen my website yet?<BR>http://www.geocities.com/lithiumeffects/pheageysfiero/<BR>Killed: '97 civic CS auto;<BR>Killed by: '94 Civic Ex, '96 Camery LX<BR>Vandalised: 2 time (in 2 days).||||reg||4
Z||000021||kneebarx||12-14-2001||01:23 PM||||whoa, glad everyone made it out okay, geez, you guys have been in some pretty bad bangers.  Thanks for all the info, I had no idea the fiero was that safe, my friend was also telling me how theress no engine in the front to protect you if you crash.  So i thought it wasnt very safe, but now i know.||||reg||1
Z||000022||MinnGreenGT||12-14-2001||02:34 PM||||Damn Double Posts!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by MinnGreenGT (edited 12-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000023||MinnGreenGT||12-14-2001||02:34 PM||||Just another note... I've purchsed (with my Dad) 2 Fieros that had been involved in accidents (both silver coupes, one hit in the front, the other in the rear- kinda funny seeing them sit next to each other).  Anyway, the one hit in the rear was hit by a fairly large truck (delivery/box truck) in the right rear corner.  I couldn't hardly get to the top of the engine!  But The Driver's door still opened & closed without a problem... that's solid engineering.<P>I also recall reading a newpaper clipping about a couple of people cruising down the road when a Tractor/Trailer came head-on across the median from the other direction.  Both the driver & passenger saw it coming and ducked!  The trailer sheared the top off the Fiero (as it would've any car)... but aside from that, the car was pretty much unscathed! & Drivable (was it the first unofficial convertible... doubt it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">)<P>------------------<BR>              <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/GreenGT6.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000024||btoth||12-14-2001||03:17 PM||||My first car was hit at the driver side tire area by a car doing 45-60mph.  I wasn't moving.  Frame got bent, wheel smashed up, and the plastic ripped off.  My friend (in the passenger seat) and I didn't feel much.  Car was totaled, no injuries.<P>Three weeks before that I rear-ended a Geo Trakker (sp?) and ripped it's hitch off.  It broke my emblem on the nose of the car and smooshed the absorber underneath.  <P>Just think of this:  there's a steel frame under all that plastic and you have a real nice crumple zone in the front and the back.  The thing to watch out for are SUVs and big trucks with their bumpers at your window.  That can be disturbing at times.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000025||Mach10||12-16-2001||10:42 PM||||<A HREF="http://www.crashtest.com/explanations/archive/crash.htm#anchor106731" TARGET=_blank>And that's all I have to say about that!</A>||||reg||10
Z||000026||northstarfieroorbust||12-18-2001||11:56 PM||||Im on my third fiero, now and i love them, i love them so much infact. I bough another one two weeks after wrapping my second one around a telephone pole. Im happy to say, that if i had hit the pole in any other car i probably would have at least been hurt fairly bad.  But with the wonders of the fiero structure, i simply opened the door and got out, a little shook up, but no worse for the wear. i say go for it, but beware. once a fiero owner youll never turn back.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fieronewrims2-2_2.gif"> Looking for an insane amount of power in a sweet little car||||reg||14
Z||000027||Will||12-19-2001||12:31 AM||||Are Fieros safe?<P>Only as safe as the driver, as someone else mentioned.<P>They are good in head-ons precisely because the engine is in the back. Think about it: a cast iron engine block won't crush or absorb energy in a crash; the open frame in your front compartment will crush, and in so doing will absorb and dissipate some of the energy of the impact.<BR>They are good in side impacts because the doors have very heavy, strong structure, as well as MASSIVE side impact beams.<BR>They are good in roll-overs because the space frame's overhead elements are very strong (I have experience with this particular aspect).<P>May post pictures of the one I flipper later.||||reg||1
Z||000028||Master_Sushi||12-19-2001||12:33 AM||||MR2's also got 5 stars in 1985. I just think the mid engine layout is safe. I honestly never feel "unsafe" when im driving in my fiero. I've has passangers freak out whenever I would get close to a car biger then my fiero (almost anything lol). I'f im stuck between a few 18-wheelers im allways gonna feel unsafe, but even if I was in a tank I still would be.||||reg||1
Z||000029||Bikemanj||12-19-2001||01:46 AM||||These pictures of wrecked Fieros make me want to cry.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> <BR>Glad everyone got out OK.<P>No fuel tank is safe when igniters are taped to it.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000030||Krashdavus||12-19-2001||10:56 AM||||Good question, and thanks to all the respondants.  I just purachased a mint '88 GT for my wife for our 10 wedding anniversary, and the safety issue is always in the back of my mind as I watch her pull away each day on her way to work.  I fell much more at ease after going through this thread.  Thanks again to everyone who provided input.||||reg||14
Z||000031||Slammed Fiero||12-19-2001||12:44 PM||||I drove mine into the back of an Aerostar @ around 40mph , he had a reese hitch , completley ruined the front fascia , Broke the rebar under the bumper , pushed the hood foreward around 6 inches... (The car just turned over 20,000 miles OUCH!)<P>I used a hacksaw to cut the headlight door  in the hood away so I could get the lights up , duct taped the hell out of it, and drove it home 3 hours from Toronto to kingston.<P>Their built like tanks! The weight of the door feels like my 79 Caddy. <P>It's not a case of them being built unsafe at all. It's the low slung design of the car , Drive into the back of a transport and your decapitated. Try to drive into things of euqal or lesser height.<P>JM ||||reg||1
Z||000032||LZeitgeist||12-19-2001||02:29 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>StuGood writes:<BR>But-! Fieros can be a real hazard to bystanders, because when you are working on them, you tend to throw tools<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>You got THAT right!!! Argh!!!||||reg||1
Z||000033||blakeinspace||12-19-2001||04:34 PM||||This one time... in band camp...<P>Actually I shoved the nose of my Formula fairly far under an F150 two years ago.  He pulled out in front of me and I t-boned him doing 50 into the point where his cab meets the bed.  Damn truck stopped a few feet from the windsheild, BUT the point is, it did stop.  After about 20 seconds of shock, I shoved the car into reverse and backed into a parking lot... leaking coolant the whole way.  I then walked away.  He got loaded into an ambulance.  (his insurance only gave 3500 for a car I had put 7000 in ((sigh)).  So what did I do?  I promptly bought another Form and lowered it an inch.  Might as well go all the way under if your gonna total my car!<P>On a side note... and I cant remember where I read this... but I DID read it.  In the sportcar category, Fiero was third on a list for cars where people were FATALLY injured in wrecks.  Corvette was 1st, followed by MR2.  I don't remember what other cars fell after.  It mentioned something about 'vette's and high speed collisions.... I think it was some State Farm paperwork I was thumbing through.  Or maybe I hit my head too hard in that collision 2 years ago.  <BR>My experience is the biggest injury is to your wallet, where you don't get near what you've put into your car, and you don't have receipts for your own time, labor, and pride.  Buy one anyway.  You'll be a member of a cult much bigger than the car.  Kinda like Mountain Dew.||||reg||1
Z||000034||Hairy_Fiero||12-19-2001||05:23 PM||||All I have to say is this about your BS comment about Fiero's being 3rd on the list of Fatally injured. It's all a bunch of  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_bs.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_bs.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_bs.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_bs.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_bs.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_bs.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_bs.gif">||||reg||13
